# Do not overwrite a live QProcess pointer

Never assign `process_ = new QProcess(...)` while a previous `QProcess` is still stored. The old process stays parented and running; its `finished()` can land on the new run's state and mix stderr into the wrong `errorMessage_`.

Disconnect, kill if running, wait, then `deleteLater` and null the pointer before creating the next process. Share that cleanup with the destructor.

```cpp
// BAD
process_ = new QProcess(this);
process_->start(ffmpegPath, arguments);

// GOOD
stopAndDiscardProcess();
process_ = new QProcess(this);
connect(process_, &QProcess::finished, this, &Klass::onProcessFinished);
process_->start(ffmpegPath, arguments);
```

Parenting is not a substitute for exclusive ownership of one running job.
